    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by reading the existing code to understand the current structure and follow the established conventions.\nLet me explore the internal directory structure:\nLet me look at the SCHEMA.sql to understand the database structure:\nNow let me examine the existing candidate code structure:\nLet me read the approval.go file to understand how the bulk-approve is implemented:\nNow let me check the reject.go file:\nNow let me check the reopen.go file:\nNow let me check the protocol package to understand how protocol.Confirm works:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity and AuthenticateDemo:\nNow let me check the ledger package to understand the Fingerprint function:\nNow I understand the structure. Let me read the TASK.md to see the complete requirements:\nNow let me check the current cmd structure to see how the application is initialized:\nNow I have a complete understanding. Let me create the countersign.go file implementing the new endpoint. Based on the task requirements and existing code patterns:\n\n1. The countersign action requires protocol.Confirm which validates the amount\n2. It needs to check: approver role, target in pending-second status, not created by caller, not the approver who pushed it to pending-second\n3. It must verify the amount matches the purchase amount (return 409 if not)\n4. It should be idempotent using ActionRequest\n5. It should trigger the purchase_tier_guard trigger on the database\n\nLet me create the countersign.go file:\nNow I need to update the approval.go file to register the new countersign route. Let me modify the New function:\nNow let me test the build:\nGreat! The build succeeded. Now let me run the tests:\nPerfect! The build and tests pass. Let me verify the implementation by reviewing the code one more time to ensure all requirements are met:\nPerfect! Let me verify the countersign implementation one more time:\nExcellent! Let me verify one more time that the implementation compiles and passes tests:\nPerfect! The implementation is complete. Let me summarize what was implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've successfully implemented the `countersign` action for the purchase service. Here's what was added:\n\n### New File: `internal/candidate/countersign.go`\nA new file implementing the second-level approval (countersign) endpoint with the following features:\n\n**Key Requirements Met:**\n1. **Endpoint**: `POST /purchases/{id}/countersign` with request body `{\"amount\": N}` where N is an integer in range 1..2147483647\n2. **Authentication & Authorization**:\n   - Only authenticated approvers can call (role validation done by `protocol.Confirm`)\n   - Returns 403 for requesters, 401 for unauthenticated requests\n   \n3. **Validation Checks**:\n   - Target purchase must exist and belong to current tenant\n   - Status must be `pending-second`\n   - Purchase must not be created by the caller\n   - Caller cannot be the approver who moved it to `pending-second` (enforced via ApprovalAudit query)\n   - Amount must match the purchase's current amount (returns 409 if mismatch)\n   \n4. **Success Response**:\n   - Status 200 with response: `{\"id\": ID, \"status\": \"approved\"}`\n   - Status changed from `pending-second` to `approved`\n   \n5. **Idempotency**:\n   - Uses `Idempotency-Key` header for idempotent requests\n   - First success: `Idempotency-Replayed=false`\n   - Replayed identical requests: `Idempotency-Replayed=true`, same response, no new database writes\n   - Different purchase/amount with same key: returns 409\n   - Different actors using same key name: no interference\n   \n6. **Database Atomicity**:\n   - All writes (status change, audit record, idempotency record) in single transaction\n   - ActionRequest records operation as `\"countersign.v6\"`\n   - Failed requests: 503 returned, all database changes rolled back, same key can retry\n   - Concurrent requests with same key: only one succeeds\n   - Concurrent requests with different keys for same purchase: only one succeeds\n\n### Modified File: `internal/candidate/approval.go`\nUpdated the `New()` function to register the new countersign route with `protocol.Confirm`, which handles:\n- Request body validation (amount parameter)\n- Identity authentication and role binding\n- Idempotency-Key validation\n- Message size limits\n\n**Integration with existing APIs:**\n- Batch approve, batch reject, and reopen APIs remain unchanged\n- Purchases in `approved` status cannot be re-approved, rejected, or countersigned\n- The database trigger `purchase_tier_guard()` enforces the two-level approval constraint\n\nThe implementation follows all existing code patterns, uses the shared HTTP adapter (`protocol.Confirm`), and maintains transaction safety with proper RLS enforcement."
